                <h2>The Dashboard Is a Service Control Center, Not a Client List</h2>
<p>Agency account-opening services commonly involve intake, document collection, platform review, account binding, funding, invoicing, balance monitoring, and campaign onboarding. When information is scattered across spreadsheets, email, and chat, teams struggle to identify where a client is blocked, while agencies may overlook low balances, permission issues, or expired documentation.</p>
<p>A practical dashboard should organize every client under a unique ID and use consistent stages: pending intake, in progress, awaiting client action, completed, and on hold. Each stage must include an owner, due date, next action, and completion criteria.</p>
<h2>1. Onboarding: Replace Repeated Follow-Ups with Checklists</h2>
<h3>Intake checks</h3>
<ul>
<li>Create a unique record for every client to avoid confusion between similarly named companies.</li>
<li>Record the legal entity name, registration region, target markets, advertising platforms, expected daily spend, and contacts.</li>
<li>Before uploading documents, check legibility, validity, consistency with the legal entity, and authorization scope.</li>
<li>Restrict access to sensitive information and control downloading or forwarding.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Progress checks</h3>
<p>Distinguish between “documents required,” “platform review,” “activation required,” and “ready for campaigns.” Operations teams should review overdue items daily, while clients should only see tasks that require action. If a submission is returned, the task should specify the missing field, submission channel, and resubmission deadline.</p>
<p>Internal response targets can be configured by team size and client type. They should reflect actual capacity rather than present an unrealistic one-size-fits-all promise.</p>
<h2>2. Account and Permissions: Verify Before Launch</h2>
<p>Account creation does not mean the account is immediately usable. Before delivery, verify:</p>
<ul>
<li>The legal entity, currency, time zone, and billing details;</li>
<li>Agency permissions and role-based access based on least privilege;</li>
<li>Clear responsibilities among sales, finance, operations, and clients;</li>
<li>Payment method, spending limit, billing cycle, and exception handling;</li>
<li>Balance, consumption, and credit-risk alerts.</li>
</ul>
<p>An anonymous tool app once opened accounts for several regions simultaneously. Currency and ownership issues were not detected until campaigns were ready to launch. Adding these fields to the delivery checklist allowed the team to resolve them before activation.</p>
<h2>3. Funding and Balances: Bring Cash Status into One View</h2>
<p>A funding record should contain the order number, amount, currency, payment status, platform credit status, credited time, invoice status, and the reason for any delay.</p>
<p>Use three alert types:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Balance alerts:</strong> Based on average daily spend and settlement cycles;</li>
<li><strong>Spend alerts:</strong> For consumption materially above the client’s plan;</li>
<li><strong>Process alerts:</strong> For completed payments not yet credited or unresolved invoices.</li>
</ol>
<p>Thresholds should be configurable by campaign cycle and client needs. A fixed amount should not automatically apply to every account.</p>
<h2>4. Campaigns and Risk Controls: Connect the Dashboard to Actual Spend</h2>
<p>Once campaigns begin, the dashboard should also show creative review, scheduling, daily budgets, actual spend, risk events, and client feedback. Key checks include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Whether creatives were rejected because of licensing, geographic, or policy restrictions;</li>
<li>Whether daily budgets are compatible with available balances;</li>
<li>Whether abnormal clicks, sudden spending spikes, or frequent payment-detail changes occurred;</li>
<li>Whether landing pages, domains, and tracking parameters match the campaign plan;</li>
<li>Whether restricted, inaccessible, or suspended accounts have triggered an assigned response.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every risk event should record the incident, scope, action, owner, and final resolution. Deleting an ad or pausing a campaign should be treated as containment, not closure.</p>
<h2>5. Reconciliation and Weekly Reviews</h2>
<p>Run a short weekly review with operations, finance, and client success teams. Answer five questions only: Which onboarding tasks are overdue? Which accounts may run low on funds? Which payments have not been credited? Which incidents remain open? Do actual campaign costs match the billing records?</p>
<p>For monthly reconciliation, organize funding, spend, rebates, and refunds by client, currency, platform, and billing period. Preserve transaction IDs, invoice numbers, and adjustment notes. Unresolved differences should enter a dispute list with an owner and supporting-document deadline.</p>
<h2>Acceptance Criteria</h2>
<p>A reliable agency account-opening dashboard allows clients to see progress, operations teams to locate blockers, finance teams to reconcile funds, and managers to identify risks. Every status change should retain an owner and timestamp. Historical records must remain auditable without allowing unlabeled, outdated client records to accumulate.</p>
<p>The purpose of a dashboard is not to create a more complicated spreadsheet. It is to connect onboarding, funding, advertising, and risk controls into a transparent, transferable, and auditable service workflow.</p>
